Myth: Before light learned to travel, the world drifted inside a black tortoise shell on a sea of rain. Villages had no roads, only songs tied from house to house, and children knew their mothers by the warmth of their hands.

In a reed hut lived Nira, a small moth-colored girl who collected the breath of dying fires in a clay bowl. The elders smiled sadly. “Ash is only yesterday,” they told her.

“Yesterday still knows the way,” Nira said.

Then came the Long Storm, beating so loudly that songs broke apart and the tortoise sank, forgetting the sky. Rivers climbed trees, stones floated, and every hearth went cold. Nira carried her bowl to the tallest reed and whispered to the coals she had saved. One by one they woke, red as berries, and rose through the rain.

Above the clouds waited the Heron of Morning, whose wings were folded around the sun. Seeing Nira’s brave sparks, he shook loose a golden feather. It fell into her bowl, became flame, and shone upon the tortoise’s brow.

The great shell opened. Mountains lifted, rivers remembered the sea, and the first dawn spilled over all things.

