Then the Great Rain arrived, louder than flutes, shells, and sleep. The tortoise lost heaven’s path and sank. Rivers climbed cedars, fish flashed through windows, stones floated like leaves, and prayers thinned until they vanished.

Nira climbed the tallest cedar, holding her cup close. At the crown she sang the lullaby for frightened children. The coals stirred, became red birds, and flew against the roof of night.

Above the storm, the Heron of Morning opened one golden wing. A feather fell, becoming flame. The tortoise saw it, lifted his brow, and cracked darkness wide.

Gold poured through. Rivers went home. Fireflies kept Nira’s sparks, and every hearth kept one small feather of dawn alive.
